# Intervention and Counterfactuals

* Interventions in Causal Networks

- @Recap@: **Causal Bayesian Networks** represent cause–effect relations
  between variables
  - E.g., $Sprinkler \to WetGrass$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **Intervention** means setting a variable to a fixed value,
  overriding its causal mechanism

- @Example@: _"Turning the sprinkler on manually"_ regardless of cloudiness
  - Replace equation $S = f_S(C, U_S)$ with $S = 1$ (set to true)
  - Causal link from $Cloudy \to Sprinkler$ is _cut_, forming a new
    "mutilated" model
::::
:::: {.column width=25%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Weather [label="Weather", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather -> Rain;
    Weather -> Sprinkler;
    Rain -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
\scriptsize \center
_Original graph_
::::
:::: {.column width=25%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Weather [label="Weather", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FF8888", xlabel="P(S | W) = True"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather -> Rain;
    Rain -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}

```
\scriptsize \center
_Mutilated graph_
::::
:::

* The Do-Operator

- @Definition@: The **do-operator** $do(X = x)$ represents performing an _action
  that sets_ $X$ to $x$ (which is different than _observing_ $X = x$)
  - $do(X_j = x_j^k)$ removes $\Pr(x_j | Parents(X_j))$ from the product
    and gives a new joint distribution:
    $$
    P_{X_j = x_j^k}(x_1, \ldots, x_n) =
    \begin{cases}
    \prod_{i \ne j} \Pr(x_i | Parents(X_i))
      & \text{if } X_j = x_j^k \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    $$

- @Remark@: Difference between **observation and intervention**
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X=x)) \ne \Pr(Y | X)$$
  - Observing $S = 1$ _provides information_ about its causes (e.g., weather,
    Markov blanket)
  - Intervening with $do(S = 1)$ _breaks_ those causal dependencies: it doesn't
    yield information about anything

* Causal Effect Estimation with Do-Operator

- @Definition@: the **adjustment formula** estimate causal effects of $X_j$ on $X_i$
  $$
  \Pr(X_i = x_i | do(X_j = x_j^k))
    = \sum_{Parents(X_j)}
        \Pr(x_i | x_j^k, Parents(X_j))
        \Pr(Parents(X_j))
  $$

- @Example@: In the Sprinkler model, $do(S=1)$ gives the new distribution:
  $$
  \Pr(c, r, w, g | do(S=1))
    = \Pr(c) \Pr(r|c) \Pr(w|r, S=1) \Pr(g|w)
  $$
  - Only descendants of $Sprinkler$ (i.e., $WetGrass$) change
  - $Weather$ and $Rain$ remain unaffected

- @Intuition@: The do-operator isolates _causal effects_ by simulating external
  manipulation, essential for answering _"what if"_ questions

* Counterfactuals

- @Definition@: A **counterfactual** describes what would have happened in the
  past under a different scenario
  - _"What would the outcome have been, if X had been different?"_

- @Examples@: Business applications
  - _"What if we had two suppliers instead of one? Would we have fewer
    delays?"_
  - _"Would customers be more satisfied if we shipped products in one week
    instead of three?"_
  - Businesses want answers to this type of questions, but correlation-based
    models can't provide them

- @Fact@: Causal reasoning goes **beyond correlation and association**
  - Requires a causal model to simulate alternate realities
  - E.g., actual: _"student received tutoring, scored 85%"_; counterfactual:
    _"without tutoring, score would be 70%"_
  - Requires strong assumptions and accurate models
  - Difficult to validate directly since counterfactuals are unobservable

* Causal Discovery

- @Definition@: **Causal discovery** learns causal network structure from data
  by identifying which variables directly cause others (i.e., causal directions,
  not just correlations)

- @Approaches@
  - _Search-based methods_: Start with empty/initial model, iteratively modify
    (add/reverse/delete links), evaluate fit to data, ensure acyclicity
  - _Constraint-based methods_: Infer causal directions from conditional
    independence tests
    - E.g., if $X \perp Y | Z$, this constrains possible arrows

- @Key idea@: Causal discovery bridges Bayesian learning and causal inference
  - Under certain assumptions, infer causality from observational data, not just
    experiments

- @Challenges@
  - Possible network structures grow super-exponentially with number of
    variables
  - Need complexity penalties to avoid overfitting

# Randomized Controlled Trial

* Randomized Controlled Trials

- @Definition@: **Randomized Controlled Trials** (RCTs) estimate causal effects
  by comparing treatment and control groups via _random assignment_ and not subject
  choice
  - E.g., assign drug or placebo, based on coin flip

- @Goal@: Estimate $\Pr(Y | do(X))$
  - Randomization simulates do-operator by removing incoming arrows to $X$
  - Eliminates known and unknown confounding paths
  - Enables causal inference without knowing causal graph
  - Ensure groups statistically equivalent except for treatment
  - Isolate treatment effect
  - Turns observational data into experimental data: $\Pr(Y | X) = \Pr(Y |
    do(X))$

- @Pros@
  - Implements intervention in principled, unbiased way
  - Foundation for scientific experimentation and evidence-based policy
  - _Gold standard_ of causal inference (when feasible)

* RCT Example: After-School Tutoring

- @Question@: Does offering after-school tutoring increase probability student
  passes end-of-term exam?

- @Algorithm@
  - Population: eligible students with proper sample size $n$
    - Treatment: $X = 1$ (tutoring offered)
    - Control: $X = 0$ (no tutoring)
  - Students randomly assigned to $X \in \{0, 1\}$
    - Randomization ensures balance on GPA, motivation, parental income, etc
  - Define $Y = 1$ if student passes exam, 0 otherwise
  - After randomization: $\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \Pr(Y | X)$ so we can measure it by
    observation
  - Report causal effect: $Y_{X=1} - Y_{X=0}$

* Randomized Controlled Trial: Limitations

- @Limitation@: There are several cases where RCTs are not possible
  1. **Practical constraints**
     - Can be expensive or logistically difficult

  2. **Ethical constraints**
     - _Harmful treatments_
       - E.g., testing if asbestos causes cancer

  3. **Implementation challenges**
     - _Non-compliance_: participants may not follow assignment
     - _Attrition_: dropout rates differ between groups
     - Limited generalization to broader populations
     - Requires careful monitoring

- @Variants@
  - _Blind RCT_: participants unaware of group assignment (e.g., placebo)
  - _Double-blind RCT_: both participants and investigators unaware of
    assignment

# Backdoor Adjustment

* Backdoor Paths: Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: Estimating price effect on sales

- @Naive Solution@:
  - Microeconomics suggests to compute the $Quantity$ sold as function of $Price$
  - This experiment is difficult to perform

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L08.3_Lesson09-Price_Quantity.png){height=30%}
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Solution@: Vary the $Quantity$ using incentives
  - $AdSpend$ is a **confounder** since it affects both price and sales
    - Higher ad budgets increase product value perception $\to$ higher price
    - Ad spend directly increases sales
    - Backdoor path: $Price \leftarrow AdSpend \to Sales$
  - Company needs to control for $AdSpend$ to isolate price effect via:
    1. Regression with $AdSpend$ as covariate
    2. Backdoor criterion

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz[width=70%]
digraph CausalDAG {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Price [label="Price", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Sales [label="Sales", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    AdSpend [label="Ad Spend", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];

    {rank=same; Price; Sales;}

    Price -> Sales;
    AdSpend -> Price;
    AdSpend -> Sales;
}
```
::::
:::

* The Back-Door Adjustment

- @Theorem@:
  - Assume:
    - Correct causal graph available
    - Can identify variables blocking backdoor paths (exact definition later)
  - The **adjustment formula** enables causal inference from observational data
    $$\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_z \Pr(Y | X, Z=z) \Pr(Z=z)$$

- @Implications@
  - Enables intervention-level inference (causality ladder level 2)
    using only observational data (level 1)
  - Alternative to randomized experiments
  - _"Correlation does imply causation"_ under specific graph conditions

* Back-Door Path

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: **Frontdoor path** is a direct causal effect $X \to Y$
  - No arrows into $X$, (so no backdoor path, no confounders)
  - Clean causal pathway
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
\vspace{1cm}

```graphviz
digraph NoBackdoorExample {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="X\n(Treatment)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Y [label="Y\n(Outcome)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; }

    X -> Y [label="causal"];
}
```
::::
:::

\vspace{0.5cm}

- @Definition@: A **backdoor path** is any path from $X$ to $Y$ starting with
  an arrow into $X
  - It must be into $X$ since otherwise it will be a causal path
  - The rest of the path can be against the direction of DAG edges
  - E.g., $X \leftarrow \cdots \leftrightarrow Y$
  - E.g., $IceCreamSales \leftarrow Season \rightarrow DrowningDeaths$
  - It creates spurious associations between $X$ and $Y$ (even if changing $X$
    doesn't affect $Y$)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Example@:
  - Confounder $Z$ creates backdoor path between $X$ and $Y$ (even if there was
    no direct path between $X$ and $Y$)
  - Path: $X \leftarrow Z \to Y$ is a backdoor path
  - Conditioning on $Z$ blocks this path
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph BackdoorExample {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Z [label="Z\n(Confounder)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    X [label="X\n(Treatment)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Y [label="Y\n(Outcome)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; }

    Z -> X [color="red", penwidth=2.5];
    Z -> Y [color="red", penwidth=2.5, label="Backdoor path", fontcolor="red"];
    X -> Y [label="causal", style="dotted"];
}
```
::::
:::

* Backdoor Criteria

- @Definition@: A set $\mathcal{Z} = {Z_1, ..., Z_n}$ satisfies the **backdoor
  criterion** relative to $X \to Y$ if:
  1. No variable in $\mathcal{Z}$ is a descendant of $X$
  2. $\mathcal{Z}$ blocks all backdoor paths from $X$ to $Y$

- @Intuition@: A set for the backdoor criteria removes spurious paths by
  controlling for common causes

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Example@: Backdoor criterion with $\mathcal{Z} = \{Z\}$
  - No element in $\mathcal{Z}$ is a descendant of $X$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=60%]
digraph SatisfiesBD {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1.4];

    Z [label="Z", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    X [label="X", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Y [label="Y", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; }

    Z -> X [color="red", penwidth=2.5];
    Z -> Y [color="red", penwidth=2.5];
    X -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Counterexample@: $\mathcal{Z} = \{M\}$ violates backdoor criterion
  - $M$ is a descendant of $X$ ✗
  - Violates criterion 1 (descendants of $X$ cannot be in $\mathcal{Z}$)

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz[width=80%]
digraph FailsBD {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1.4];

    Z [label="Z", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    X [label="X", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    M [label="M", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Y [label="Y", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank=same; X; M; Y; }

    Z -> X [color="red", penwidth=2.5];
    Z -> Y [color="red", penwidth=2.5];
    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

* Chains, Forks, and Colliders

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Chain@: $X \to M \to Y$
  - Conditioning on $M$ blocks causal effect
  - Keep mediators unconditioned
  - **Do not condition**
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph CausalFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="X"];
    M [fillcolor="#F4A6A6", label="M"];
    Y [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Y"];

    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

\vspace{1.0cm}

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Fork@: $X \leftarrow Z \to Y$ (confounder)
  - Conditioning on $Z$ removes confounding
  - **Must condition**
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz[width=60%]
digraph CausalDAG {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    C [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    D [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    {rank=same; C; D;}

    X -> C;
    X -> D;
}
```
::::
:::

\vspace{0.5cm}

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Collider@: $X \to M \leftarrow Y$
  - Conditioning on $M$ introduces bias
  - Keep colliders unconditioned
  - **Do not condition**
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz[width=60%]
digraph ColliderExample {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="X", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Y [label="Y", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Z [label="Z", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];

    {rank=same; X; Y;}

    X -> Z;
    Y -> Z;
}
```
::::
:::

* Common Mistakes in Back-Door Adjustment

- @Key idea@: Backdoor criterion specifies _exactly_ which variables to
  condition on and not condition on to transform observation into intervention

- @Error@: Before the backdoor criterion was formalized, researchers
  "conditioned on everything"
  - **Conditioning on everything is wrong!**

- @Consequence@: Thousands of published papers across medicine, economics, social
  science use _incorrect observational methods_

- Other **common errors** are:
  - Conditioning on descendant of $X$ biases estimate
  - Controlling too many variables opens colliders, introduces bias
  - Missing backdoor paths creates spurious confounding
  - Conditioning on mediators blocks causal effect
  - Ignoring unobserved confounders makes effect unidentifiable

* When Back-Door Adjustment Fails

- @Limitations@: Backdoor criterion not universally applicable
  - No observable set satisfies criterion if unobserved confounders exist
  - Requires accurate causal graph, but it is easy to omit variables

- @Alternatives@
  - _Frontdoor criterion_: uses mediators, handles unobserved confounders
  - _Instrumental variables_: uses external variation
  - _Do-calculus_: symbolic transformations to eliminate $do()$

# Frontdoor Adjustment

* Front-Door Adjustment: Definition

- @Definition@: **Frontdoor criterion** identifies causal effects when
  unobserved confounders exist, but a _mediator_ transmits all influence from
  treatment to outcome

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- Graph structure:
  - $X$: treatment
  - $M$: mediator
  - $Y$: outcome
  - $U$: unobserved confounder
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph CerealAds {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    U [label="U", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    X [label="X", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    M [label="M", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    Y [label="Y", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; M }

    U -> X;
    U -> Y;
    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Assumptions@
  1. All directed paths from $X$ to $Y$ pass through $M$
  2. No unobserved confounder affects both $X$ and $M$
  3. All backdoor paths from $M$ to $Y$ blocked by $X$

- @Formula@: Estimate $\Pr(Y | do(X))$ despite unobserved $U$
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_m \Pr(M | X) \sum_{x'} \Pr(Y | M, X') \Pr(X')$$

* Cereal and Ads: Does Advertising Cause Sales?

- @Question@: Does watching ads $(X)$ cause cereal purchases $(Y)$?
  - Naïve answer: yes, obviously!
  - **But wait...**

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Hidden confounder@: _"Parents who care about breakfast"_ $(U)$
  - Enable more TV time (increases ad exposure)
  - Buy more cereal regardless
  - Creates spurious correlation

- Observing $X$ and $Y$ without controlling $U$ yields spurious association
  - Same phenomenon: ice cream sales and drowning deaths

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph CerealAds {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="X\n(Watch ads)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    U [label="U\n(Care about breakfast)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    Y [label="Y\n(Buy cereal)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; }

    U -> X;
    U -> Y;
    X -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Problem@: businesses spend on ads that don't actually drive sales

* Cereal and Ads: Three Solution Strategies

- @Strategy 1: Backdoor adjustment@
  - If can observe and measure $U$ (parent breakfast attitudes), control for it
  - Compare families with identical attitudes, see if ads change buying
  - Requires knowing and measuring the confounder

- @Strategy 2: Randomization@
  - Randomized experiments break $U \to X$ link
  - Random ad assignment removes selection bias
  - Differences in purchase arise purely from ads
  - Gold standard for causal inference

- @Strategy 3: Frontdoor adjustment@
  - Works without observing $U$, using only observable mediator

* Front-Door Mediator: Kids Nagging

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- @Mechanism@: Ads work by making kids nag for cereal $(M)$
  - Real advertising strategy: kids see ads, ask parents for product
  - Retail displays kids at eye level (e.g., candy at checkout)
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L08.3.Nagging_kids.png)
::::
:::

\vspace{0.5cm}

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Causal chain@:
  - Ads $(X)$ $\to$ Kids nag $(M)$ $\to$ Parents buy cereal $(Y)$

- @Structure satisfies frontdoor@
  - Unobserved $U$ affects $Y$ but _not_ $M$
  - Hidden breakfast-caring attitude doesn't cause nagging: only ads do
  - Can estimate $\Pr(Y | do(X))$ without observing $U$
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph CerealAds {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    U [label="U\n(Care about breakfast)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    X [label="X\n(Watch ads)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    M [label="M\n(Kids nag)", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    Y [label="Y\n(Buy cereal)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; M }

    U -> X;
    U -> Y;
    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

* Applying Front-Door: Does It Work?

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Question@: Does kids' nagging satisfy frontdoor?

- @Answer@
  - All ads influence flows through nagging ($X \to M \to Y$)
  - No confounder affects both ads and nagging (TV schedule random)
  - All confounding between nagging and buying blocked by controlling ads
  - **Yes, frontdoor criterion holds**

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph CerealAds {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    U [label="U\n(Care about breakfast)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    X [label="X\n(Watch ads)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    M [label="M\n(Kids nag)", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    Y [label="Y\n(Buy cereal)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    { rank=same; X; Y; M }

    U -> X;
    U -> Y;
    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Solution@: Estimate $\Pr(Y | do(X))$ from observational data alone
  1. Observe: How often ads cause nagging $\Pr(M | X)$
  2. Observe: How nagging changes buying $\Pr(Y | M, X')$
  3. Combine:
     $$\Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_m \Pr(M | X) \sum_{x'} \Pr(Y | M, X') \Pr(X')$$

# Do-Calculus

* Do-Calculus: Formal Framework

- @Definition@: **Do-calculus** provides formal rules for reasoning about causal
  effects in graphical models (Pearl, 2000)

- @Problem@
  - **We want**: Causal effects $\Pr(Y | do(X = x))$ (intervention distribution)
  - **We have**: Observational data $\Pr(Y | X = x)$ (conditional distribution)
  - **Gap**: Generally $\Pr(Y | X) \ne \Pr(Y | do(X))$ due to confounding

- @Solution@: Do-calculus transforms intervention expressions into observational
  terms computable from data, given graph structure

* Do-Calculus: Three Rules
- @Goal@: Systematically reduce $do()$ expressions to observable terms when graph
  structure permits

- @Rule 1: Insertion/Deletion of Observations@
  - If $Y \perp Z | X, W$ in $G_{\overline{X}}$ (remove incoming edges to $X$):
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X), Z, W) = \Pr(Y | do(X), W)$$

- @Rule 2: Action/Observation Exchange@
  - If $Y \perp Z | X, W$ in $G_{\overline{X}, \underline{Z}}$ (remove incoming
    to $X$, outgoing from $Z$):
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X), do(Z), W) = \Pr(Y | do(X), Z, W)$$

- @Rule 3: Insertion/Deletion of Actions@
  - If $Y \perp Z | X, W$ in $G_{\overline{X}, \overline{Z(W)}}$ (remove
    incoming to $X$ and to $Z$ except from $W$):
  $$\Pr(Y | do(X), do(Z), W) = \Pr(Y | do(X), W)$$

* Back/Front-Door as Applications of Do-Calculus

- @Relationship@: Backdoor and frontdoor are **special cases** of do-calculus
  - Simpler graphical conditions allowing $\Pr(Y | do(X))$ from observational
    data

- @Backdoor adjustment@: If $\mathcal{Z}$ blocks all backdoor paths $X \to Y$
  (paths into $X$), then:
  $$
  \Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_z \Pr(Y | X, Z) \Pr(Z)
  $$

- @Frontdoor adjustment@: If mediator $Z$ satisfies:
  1. $X \to Z \to Y$ (all influence through $Z$)
  2. No confounder affects $X$ and $Z$
  3. Backdoor paths $Z \to Y$ blocked by $X$

  then:
  $$
  \Pr(Y | do(X)) = \sum_z \Pr(Z | X) \sum_{x'} \Pr(Y | Z, X') \Pr(X')
  $$
